## Why This Is Asked

Tests dynamic runtime schemas, non-blocking fetch, and routing on validation failure in KCNA pipelines.

## Key Concepts

- KCNA message with schemaId and payload
- Runtime schema fetch and TTL cache
- Per-message versioned validation and routing
- Safe error handling without blocking processing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic runtime validator setup using a cache and Ajv
class SchemaCache {
  constructor(fetcher, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.fetcher = fetcher; this.ttl = ttlMs; this.store = new Map();
  }
  async get(version) {
    const entry = this.store.get(version);
    const now = Date.now();
    if (entry && (now - entry.ts) < this.ttl) return entry.validate;
    const schema = await this.fetcher(version);
    const Ajv = require('ajv');
    const ajv = new Ajv();
    const validate = ajv.compile(schema);
    this.store.set(version, { ts: now, validate });
    return validate;
  }
}

// usage sketch
const cache = new SchemaCache(async (v) => fetch(`/schemas/events/${v}.json`).then(r => r.json()));
async function onMessage(msg) {
  const { schemaId, payload } = msg.value;
  const validate = await cache.get(schemaId);
  const ok = validate(payload);
  if (ok) publish('validated_events', payload); else publish('invalid_events', payload);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test schema evolution with multiple concurrent versions?
- How would you handle schema fetch failures and guard against cache stampedes?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates real-world stateful Kafka Streams processing with a TTL-backed side-input, focusing on correctness under cache miss, update, and eviction scenarios, as well as robust testing strategies. It also assesses the ability to design APIs and observability for per-user enrichment in a high-throughput pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- TTL-based caching and eviction semantics
- Side-input-driven cache updates
- Per-message offset guarantees and at-least-once delivery
- Idempotent emits and deduplication

## Code Example

```java
// Implementation sketch
class FraudScoringSink implements KafkaSink {
  private final LoadingCache<String, RiskProfile> cache;

  public FraudScoringSink() {
    this.cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
      .expireAfterWrite(10, TimeUnit.MINUTES)
      .build(CacheLoader.from(this::fetchFromStore));
  }

  @StreamListener
  public void onTransaction(Transaction txn) {
    RiskProfile profile = cache.getIfPresent(txn.userId());
    if (profile == null) {
      profile = RiskProfile.unknown();
    }
    emit(txn.enrich(profile));
  }

  @StreamListener
  public void onFraudSignal(FraudSignal signal) {
    cache.put(signal.userId(), signal.toProfile());
  }
}
```

## Testing Strategy

```java
@Test
void cacheMissEmitsUnknownAndReEnrichesOnSignal() {
  store.onTransaction(tx("u1", 100));
  verify(emit).calledWith(risk="unknown");

  store.onFraudSignal(signal("u1", 0.9, "velocity"));
  verify(emit).calledWith(risk="high");
}

@Test
void ttlEvictionClearsStaleRiskData() {
  cache.put("u1", profile(0.8));
  clock.advance(11, MINUTES);
  cache.cleanUp();
  assertNull(cache.getIfPresent("u1"));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical stateful streaming concepts: windowed aggregations, per-tenant isolation, watermark-based late data handling, and exactly-once guarantees across rebalances. It also tests testing discipline and observability planning.

## Key Concepts

- Stateful windowed aggregations with per-(tenantId, userId) keys
- Watermarks and late-arrival handling (120s)
- Reb alo nce-safe state partitioning and eviction
- Exactly-once or idempotent sinks with proper offsets
- Observability and test strategy for late data and rebalancing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: window state store and onEvent flow
class WindowAggregator {
  constructor(windowMs, watermarkMs) { /* init stores */ }
  onEvent(e) {
    // e = {tenantId, userId, ts, value}
    const ws = Math.floor(e.ts / this.windowMs) * this.windowMs;
    const key = `${e.tenantId}:${e.userId}:${ws}`;
    let bucket = this.state.get(key) || {sum: 0, count: 0, maxTs: 0};
    bucket.sum += e.value;
    bucket.count += 1;
    bucket.maxTs = Math.max(bucket.maxTs, e.ts);
    this.state.set(key, bucket);
    // emit when window closes or on a timer
  }
}
```

